window.BCA_UNIT_CONCEPT_NOTES = {
  architecture: [
    [
      {title:"Digital signals and binary logic",notes:"A digital system represents information with a small set of distinct levels. Binary uses two logical values: 0 and 1. A bit is one binary digit, and groups of bits can represent numbers, characters or control information. Boolean algebra describes combinations of these values: AND, OR and NOT are the basic operations. A truth table lists the output for every possible input combination.",example:"For A=1 and B=0, A AND B is 0, A OR B is 1, and NOT B is 1.",exam:"Define the 0/1 levels, name the operation, and show its truth table or expression."},
      {title:"Stored-program (Von Neumann) organization",notes:"The Von Neumann model stores program instructions and data in the same main memory. A processor fetches an instruction, interprets it and performs its operation. The same stored-program idea lets a general-purpose computer run different programs without rewiring its circuits. In the basic model, instructions and data share communication paths, which can limit throughput.",example:"A program instruction and the value it adds can both be stored as bit patterns in memory.",exam:"State where instructions and data are held, then describe the fetch-and-execute role of the CPU."},
      {title:"Number systems and conversion",notes:"A positional number system assigns each digit a weight based on its place and base. Binary uses powers of 2, octal powers of 8, decimal powers of 10 and hexadecimal powers of 16. Convert to decimal by summing digit × place value. Binary-to-octal conversion groups bits in threes; binary-to-hexadecimal groups them in fours, padding the outside group with zeroes when necessary.",example:"101101₂ = 1×32 + 0×16 + 1×8 + 1×4 + 0×2 + 1×1 = 45₁₀.",exam:"Write the place values or show bit groups; include the base on every number."},
      {title:"Karnaugh maps and Boolean simplification",notes:"A Karnaugh map is a visual form of a truth table arranged in Gray-code order, so adjacent cells differ in one variable. For sum of products, place 1s and group adjacent 1-cells; for product of sums, group 0-cells. Group sizes must be powers of two. Make groups as large as possible, and remember that opposite edges wrap around as neighbors. A variable that changes within a group disappears from the simplified term.",example:"For F(A,B,C)=Σm(1,3,5,7), all marked cells have C=1, so the four-cell group simplifies to F=C.",exam:"Label the map, place values, circle legal groups, then show which variables remain."},
      {title:"Gray code, character codes and parity",notes:"Gray code orders values so adjacent codes differ by only one bit, reducing ambiguity while a value changes. Character codes assign binary patterns to letters and symbols; the guide names ASCII and EBCDIC. A parity bit is added so the total number of 1s is even or odd. A parity mismatch can detect some transmission errors, especially an odd number of flipped bits, but parity cannot locate or repair the error.",example:"If a data word has an even number of 1s, even parity adds 0; odd parity adds 1.",exam:"Explain what the code represents and state the limitation of parity detection."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Combinational and sequential circuits",notes:"A combinational circuit has no memory: its output is determined only by its current inputs. Adders, multiplexers and decoders are examples. A sequential circuit has stored state, so its output can depend on present inputs and what was stored earlier. Flip-flops and counters are sequential circuits. To classify a circuit, ask whether two identical current inputs can produce different outputs because the circuit remembers different past states.",example:"A decoder responds to its input code now; a counter remembers its current count before advancing.",exam:"Define the dependency and give one correct example of each type."},
      {title:"Half adders and full adders",notes:"A half adder adds two one-bit values. Its sum is A XOR B and its carry is A AND B. It has no carry-in, so it cannot by itself add the carry from a previous bit position. A full adder takes A, B and carry-in, then produces sum and carry-out. Full adders can be connected in sequence to add multi-bit binary numbers.",example:"1+1 gives sum 0 and carry 1, so the two-bit result is 10₂.",exam:"Compare number of inputs, carry-in, outputs and where each adder is useful."},
      {title:"Multiplexers and decoders",notes:"A multiplexer (MUX) is a controlled selector: select lines choose one of several data inputs and route it to one output. With n select bits, it can choose among up to 2ⁿ inputs. A decoder takes an input code and activates the output line represented by that code. Both route information, but one chooses an input while the other identifies an output.",example:"A 4-to-1 MUX uses two select bits to choose one of four data inputs.",exam:"Draw or describe inputs, select/code lines and output; do not swap their jobs."},
      {title:"Flip-flops store one bit",notes:"A flip-flop is a clocked storage element that holds one bit of state. The clock determines when an input can change the stored value. A D flip-flop copies D at its active clock event; an SR flip-flop has set and reset controls; JK and T flip-flops support other state changes, including toggle behavior. State the input rule and clock behavior when explaining a type.",example:"A D flip-flop with D=1 stores 1 at the active clock edge and keeps it until a later update.",exam:"Name its stored bit, input behavior and triggering clock event."},
      {title:"Registers and shift registers",notes:"A register is a group of flip-flops that stores a multi-bit value. A 4-bit register can hold one of 16 binary patterns. A shift register moves stored bits one place left or right on clock events; depending on its design, bits can enter or leave serially or in parallel. Registers are used for temporary CPU values, data movement and control.",example:"Shifting 1011 right by one position with a 0 entering on the left gives 0101.",exam:"State the width, direction of movement, input style and clock action."},
      {title:"Counters and clocking",notes:"A counter is a sequential circuit that moves through a set of states in a regular order, often binary 000, 001, 010 and so on. A synchronous counter gives all flip-flops a shared clock, so state changes are coordinated. An asynchronous or ripple counter lets one stage trigger the next; propagation delay can accumulate through the stages. The unit guide expects this timing distinction.",example:"A 3-bit binary counter has eight states, from 000 through 111, before returning to 000.",exam:"Compare clock connections and mention the ripple counter's accumulated delay."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"CPU registers",notes:"Registers provide very fast working storage inside the processor. The program counter (PC) holds the address used for the next instruction fetch. The instruction register (IR) holds the instruction being decoded or executed. The memory address register (MAR) identifies a memory location; the memory data register (MDR) carries data moving to or from memory. An accumulator or general-purpose register holds operands and results.",example:"To read memory location 120, the CPU can place 120 in the MAR and receive the fetched value through the MDR.",exam:"Name each register and describe the information it carries, not just its abbreviation."},
      {title:"Fetch, decode, execute and write-back",notes:"The PC supplies an instruction address. During fetch, the instruction is read into the IR and the PC advances. During decode, the control unit identifies the operation, operands and addressing method. During execute, the ALU, memory or a device performs the operation. The result is written to a register or memory. A branch can replace the normal next PC value; an interrupt can temporarily transfer control elsewhere.",example:"For ADD R1,R2, fetch the instruction, decode the register names, add their values in the ALU, then store the result.",exam:"Write the stages in order and connect each one to the relevant register or unit."},
      {title:"Addressing modes locate operands",notes:"An addressing mode tells the CPU where an instruction's operand comes from. Immediate addressing includes the value in the instruction. Direct addressing gives the operand's memory address. Indirect addressing points to a location that contains the actual address. Register addressing names a CPU register. The same operation can use different modes, changing where the processor obtains its data.",example:"ADD #5,R1 uses an immediate value 5; ADD R2,R1 uses the value held in R2.",exam:"For each mode, say whether the instruction contains a value, address, pointer or register name."},
      {title:"RISC and CISC design tendencies",notes:"RISC traditionally favors a smaller set of simpler, regular instructions, which can make decoding and pipelining more predictable. CISC traditionally offers a larger set of more complex instructions, sometimes combining several operations. These describe design tendencies rather than strict modern categories: processors may translate complex instructions into simpler internal operations. Compare instruction style and tradeoffs without claiming one label means every CPU behaves identically.",example:"A RISC-style instruction may load a value, then use a separate add; a CISC-style instruction may combine more work in one instruction.",exam:"Compare instruction complexity, format and implementation tendency; mention that real designs can blend ideas."},
      {title:"Stack organization",notes:"A stack is last-in, first-out (LIFO) storage: the most recently pushed item is the first popped. A stack pointer identifies the current top. CPUs use stacks for return addresses, temporary values, saved registers and function parameters. A function call can save where to return, do its work and restore that address when it finishes.",example:"Push A, then B; the next pop returns B before A.",exam:"Define LIFO and connect the stack pointer to calls or temporary storage."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Instruction pipelining",notes:"Pipelining divides instruction work into stages and overlaps different instructions in those stages. Once the pipeline is full, several instructions can make progress at once, improving throughput. It does not necessarily shorten the latency of one instruction. Ideal speedup assumes balanced stages and no interruptions; real pipelines lose time to startup, draining and hazards.",example:"While instruction 1 executes, instruction 2 can decode and instruction 3 can be fetched.",exam:"Explain overlap, then distinguish throughput from the time for one instruction."},
      {title:"Pipeline hazards and stalls",notes:"A structural hazard occurs when two stages need the same hardware resource at once. A data hazard occurs when an instruction needs a value that an earlier instruction has not produced yet. A control hazard follows a branch whose next address is not yet known. The processor may stall, forward a value or predict a branch; these measures reduce but do not erase the cost.",example:"If an instruction adds R1 and the next instruction immediately uses the new R1, the second may need to wait or receive a forwarded result.",exam:"Name the hazard, show the dependency or conflict and describe its effect on the pipeline."},
      {title:"Memory hierarchy and locality",notes:"Storage is arranged in levels: registers and cache are small and fast; main memory is larger but slower; secondary storage is much larger and slower again. This hierarchy balances speed and capacity. It works well because programs show locality: temporal locality means recent items may be reused, while spatial locality means nearby addresses may be accessed soon.",example:"A loop repeatedly using the same array section may benefit from both temporal and spatial locality.",exam:"Draw the levels in order and explain why size, speed and cost change across them."},
      {title:"Cache, hits and misses",notes:"A cache keeps copies of blocks from main memory near the CPU. On a hit, the needed block is already in cache; on a miss, it must be fetched from a slower level. The hit rate is hits divided by total accesses. Cache blocks exploit locality; when space is full, a replacement rule selects a block to remove. More cache can help, but its design and access patterns matter.",example:"If 90 of 100 requests hit in cache, the hit rate is 90%.",exam:"Define cache, hit, miss and hit rate; connect performance to locality."},
      {title:"Programmed I/O, interrupts and DMA",notes:"With programmed I/O, the CPU repeatedly checks device status and moves data itself, which can waste time waiting. Interrupt-driven I/O lets a device signal when service is needed. Direct Memory Access (DMA) uses a controller to move a block between a device and memory after the CPU sets up the transfer. The CPU is notified when the block is done, making DMA useful for larger transfers.",example:"A disk can signal an interrupt when ready; for a large file block, DMA can transfer data while the CPU does other work.",exam:"Compare who checks, who moves the data and how much CPU time is occupied."},
      {title:"Booth's signed multiplication",notes:"Booth's algorithm multiplies signed binary numbers represented in two's complement. It examines the current multiplier bit together with the previous bit: 01 indicates add the multiplicand, 10 indicates subtract it, and 00 or 11 means no add/subtract. After the operation, an arithmetic right shift preserves the sign. Repeat for the required number of bits. Keep the register layout and sign extension consistent.",example:"For each multiplier-bit pair, write the add/subtract/no-op decision before shifting; this prevents losing the sign.",exam:"Show the initial registers, bit-pair decision, arithmetic shift and final product."}
    ]
  ],
  evs: [
    [
      {title:"Earth's environmental spheres",notes:"The atmosphere is the layer of gases around Earth; the hydrosphere includes water; the lithosphere is the solid outer land; and the biosphere includes living organisms and the regions where life exists. These spheres interact rather than working alone. Rain moves between air, land and water; plants depend on soil and water and change the atmosphere through photosynthesis.",example:"Rain falls from the atmosphere, enters soil in the lithosphere and feeds plants in the biosphere.",exam:"Name the spheres, define each and give one interaction."},
      {title:"Natural resources",notes:"Natural resources are materials and conditions people use, including land, soil, forests, minerals, water and energy. They support food, shelter, health and economic activity. A resource can be renewable or non-renewable, but both can be damaged by careless use. Soil fertility, clean water and healthy forests also depend on ecological processes that take time to recover.",example:"A forest supplies timber and habitat, stores carbon, protects soil and helps regulate water.",exam:"Classify resource examples and mention their uses and limits."},
      {title:"Renewable and non-renewable resources",notes:"A renewable resource can replenish on a human timescale, such as sunlight or a forest that is allowed to regrow. Renewal is conditional: harvesting faster than regeneration depletes the resource. A non-renewable resource forms far more slowly than people consume it, as with many minerals and fossil fuels. Resource planning compares supply, use, impacts and alternatives.",example:"A fish stock may renew if catches stay within its ability to reproduce; overfishing can still collapse it.",exam:"Define each term and explain why renewable does not mean unlimited."},
      {title:"How resource use changes ecosystems",notes:"Deforestation removes habitat and can increase erosion. Mining changes land and may create waste or pollution. Dams alter river flow, sediment and habitats. Over-extraction of groundwater lowers water tables, while land-use change can fragment ecosystems. Effects depend on scale, location, design and management, so describe both likely benefits and possible costs rather than treating every project as identical.",example:"A dam can provide electricity and water storage while changing fish movement and displacing households.",exam:"Trace activity → environmental change → effect on people and ecosystems."},
      {title:"Sustainability and environmental equity",notes:"Sustainability means meeting present needs while protecting the conditions that let future generations meet theirs. Intergenerational equity asks whether today's choices unfairly reduce future options. Intragenerational equity asks whether benefits, costs and access are shared fairly among people living now. Environmental decisions should consider who uses a resource, who bears pollution and who participates in planning.",example:"Groundwater pumping may help current farms but leave less water for nearby households and future users.",exam:"Define sustainability and distinguish the two forms of equity with an example."},
      {title:"Assessing a large dam",notes:"A dam may store water, generate electricity and reduce some flood risks. It may also submerge land, change river flow and sediment, affect fish or wetlands, and displace households or tribal communities. Good assessment compares alternatives, consults affected people, plans fair resettlement and rehabilitation, protects ecological flows and checks long-term outcomes.",example:"Compare a large dam with smaller storage, watershed repair or demand management before selecting a plan.",exam:"Balance benefits and costs; include consultation, rehabilitation and monitoring."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Biotic and abiotic parts",notes:"An ecosystem includes biotic parts (living organisms) and abiotic conditions (light, water, air, soil, temperature and nutrients). These factors affect which organisms can survive and how they interact. A pond, forest or grassland is an ecosystem when its organisms and physical surroundings are studied together as a functioning system.",example:"In a pond, fish and algae are biotic; dissolved oxygen, sunlight and water temperature are abiotic.",exam:"Give examples from both groups and show how they influence one another."},
      {title:"Producers, consumers and decomposers",notes:"Producers such as plants and algae capture energy, usually from sunlight, and build organic food. Consumers get energy by eating producers or other consumers. Decomposers, including many fungi and bacteria, break down dead organisms and waste, returning nutrients to the environment. These roles connect food production, feeding and nutrient reuse.",example:"Grass is a producer, a grasshopper is a primary consumer, a frog can be a secondary consumer and fungi decompose remains.",exam:"Name each role and explain its function in an ecosystem."},
      {title:"Trophic levels and energy flow",notes:"A trophic level is a feeding position, such as producer, primary consumer or predator. Energy moves from one level to another through feeding, but organisms use much of it for life processes and release heat. Less usable energy is available at higher levels, which limits how many levels a food chain can support. Energy flows through the system rather than cycling back.",example:"A hawk receives only part of the energy stored in the animals it eats.",exam:"Draw arrows from food to eater and explain why energy decreases up the chain."},
      {title:"Food chains, food webs and ecological pyramids",notes:"A food chain is one path showing who eats whom. A food web links many chains because organisms often have several food sources and predators. Pyramids summarize the number of organisms, their biomass or available energy at each trophic level. Energy pyramids are upright because usable energy decreases at each transfer; other pyramid shapes can vary by ecosystem and measure.",example:"Grass → insect → frog is one chain; adding birds and snakes creates connected paths in a web.",exam:"Distinguish chain from web and identify what each pyramid measures."},
      {title:"Biodiversity and ecosystem services",notes:"Biodiversity includes variety within species, among species and across ecosystems. It supports processes such as pollination, soil formation, water regulation and nutrient cycling, which also support human livelihoods. Ecosystem services describe benefits people receive from functioning ecosystems. Losing species or habitats can weaken these processes and make communities less resilient.",example:"Pollinating insects support reproduction in many flowering plants and crops.",exam:"Explain biodiversity at more than one level and connect it to an ecosystem service."},
      {title:"Threats and conservation methods",notes:"Major threats in the guide include habitat loss, overuse, pollution, invasive species and climate change. In-situ conservation protects organisms in their natural habitats through approaches such as protected areas. Ex-situ conservation protects them outside those habitats, such as in seed banks, botanical gardens or managed breeding programs. In-situ protects ecological relationships; ex-situ can support recovery when a wild population is in severe danger.",example:"Protecting a wetland is in-situ; storing seeds from its plants is ex-situ.",exam:"Compare where protection happens, what it preserves and how the methods can work together."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Sustainable development",notes:"Sustainable development balances environmental protection, social wellbeing and economic activity. A proposal should be judged by more than output or profit: consider health, livelihoods, fairness, resource use and long-term effects. The balance does not mean every impact disappears; it means decisions identify impacts, compare alternatives and reduce harm while meeting real needs.",example:"A factory plan should consider jobs alongside water use, emissions, worker safety and local health.",exam:"Name the environmental, social and economic dimensions and apply them to a case."},
      {title:"Business responsibility and sustainable practice",notes:"Businesses can measure energy, water, materials, waste and emissions, then improve efficiency and prevent pollution at its source. They can reduce packaging, design durable products, reuse or recycle materials, choose suppliers responsibly and protect workers and nearby communities. Clear reporting makes progress checkable. Sustainability works best when it affects design and daily operations rather than being only a publicity message.",example:"A company can track water used per product, repair leaks, reuse process water where safe and report the reduction.",exam:"Give practical actions across measurement, prevention, design and accountability."},
      {title:"Environmental laws named in the guide",notes:"The unit names three Indian laws and their broad aims: the Water (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act, 1974 addresses water pollution; the Air (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act, 1981 addresses air pollution; and the Environment (Protection) Act, 1986 provides a broader framework for protecting and improving the environment. These are introductory purposes; detailed rules and amendments should be checked in current official text.",example:"For an exam overview, pair each Act's name and year with its broad environmental purpose.",exam:"Learn the full name, year and purpose; avoid claiming every compliance detail from memory."},
      {title:"Environmental assessment and affected communities",notes:"Environmental assessment identifies likely effects of a project before major decisions are finalized. It can examine land, water, air, biodiversity, health, livelihoods and social impacts; compare alternatives; and propose avoidance, reduction, rehabilitation and monitoring. Consultation helps include people who may be displaced or affected. Resettlement, rehabilitation and compensation need fair planning, not just a brief mention after construction begins.",example:"For a road project, map homes and habitats, compare route options, consult residents and monitor noise and water impacts.",exam:"Organize the answer as project → impacts → alternatives → safeguards → consultation and follow-up."},
      {title:"Climate action missions in India",notes:"The guide lists eight National Action Plan on Climate Change missions: solar energy, enhanced energy efficiency, sustainable habitat, water, sustaining the Himalayan ecosystem, Green India, sustainable agriculture and strategic knowledge for climate change. Learn the scope of each in the wording used by your course. Together they connect energy, cities, water, ecosystems, farming and research.",example:"A city energy-efficiency program connects climate planning with buildings and everyday electricity use.",exam:"Recall all eight mission themes and explain one with a concrete example."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Pollution: source, exposure, effect and control",notes:"Pollution occurs when harmful substances or energy enter the environment at levels that damage health or ecosystems. A useful way to study any type is to follow four links: identify the source, how people or organisms are exposed, what effects occur, and what control can reduce the risk. Prevention and monitoring help stop a source before harm spreads.",example:"Vehicle emissions are a source; breathing polluted air is exposure; respiratory harm is an effect; cleaner transport is a control.",exam:"Use the four-link structure and give a specific example."},
      {title:"Major forms of pollution",notes:"Air pollution can come from transport, industry, power generation, dust and burning. Water pollution can come from sewage, chemicals or runoff; soil pollution can follow waste and contaminants. Noise pollution is unwanted sound at harmful levels. Marine pollution includes materials and chemicals entering seas. For each, name its pathway and affected people or ecosystems rather than using a generic definition only.",example:"Fertilizer runoff can carry nutrients into water, encourage excessive algal growth and lower oxygen for aquatic life.",exam:"Name source, medium, receptor and effect for the pollution type asked."},
      {title:"Greenhouse effect, warming, ozone depletion and acid rain",notes:"The greenhouse effect is a natural heat-trapping process that keeps Earth warm; extra greenhouse gases strengthen it. Global warming is the long-term rise in average temperature driven strongly by increased greenhouse-gas emissions. Ozone depletion is thinning of stratospheric ozone associated with specific ozone-depleting substances. Acid rain forms when certain air pollutants react in the atmosphere and return in precipitation. These are related atmospheric issues, not synonyms.",example:"More greenhouse gases increase heat retained in the lower atmosphere; they do not cause the same process as ozone depletion.",exam:"Define each term separately and state its cause or atmospheric role."},
      {title:"Waste hierarchy and safe disposal",notes:"The waste hierarchy prioritizes preventing waste, then reducing it, reusing products, recycling materials, recovering useful value and safely disposing of what remains. Moving higher in the hierarchy usually avoids more resource use and pollution. Sorting at the source helps, but recycling cannot replace reducing unnecessary material in the first place.",example:"Bring a reusable bottle (prevent single-use waste), refill it (reuse), and recycle it when it can no longer be used.",exam:"Write the hierarchy in order and explain why prevention comes first."},
      {title:"Disaster management and resilience",notes:"Disaster management includes risk reduction before an event, preparedness to respond, emergency response during and after impact, and recovery. Risk depends on the hazard and how exposed or vulnerable people and systems are. Businesses can protect workers, infrastructure, data and supply chains with plans, clear communication, safe facilities and recovery arrangements.",example:"A flood plan can map safe routes, protect equipment above likely water levels and set a way to contact staff.",exam:"Describe the cycle: mitigation/risk reduction, preparedness, response and recovery."}
    ]
  ],
  english: [
    [
      {title:"Roots, bases and word families",notes:"A root or base carries the central meaning of a word. Related words often form a family around that base, which helps you recognize meaning and grammatical role. For example, act, action, active and actively share a related core idea but do different jobs in sentences. A dictionary entry or sentence context helps confirm which form fits.",example:"In “The actor acted actively,” act is the base idea; actor names a person and actively describes how an action happens.",exam:"Identify the base and explain how the related word changes meaning or word class."},
      {title:"Prefixes and suffixes",notes:"A prefix is attached before a base and often changes meaning: re- in rewrite means again, and un- in unfair reverses or negates a quality. A suffix follows the base and can change meaning or grammatical role: teach becomes teacher; kind becomes kindness. Watch spelling changes such as dropping or changing a final letter, and check the resulting word in context.",example:"Help → helpful → unhelpful: -ful means full of; un- reverses the positive quality.",exam:"Show base + affix → new word and state the meaning."},
      {title:"Synonyms, antonyms and shades of meaning",notes:"Synonyms have similar meanings; antonyms express contrast. Synonyms are rarely identical in every context because tone, formality and common word partners can differ. “Begin a project” and “start a project” are both natural, while a synonym for “large” may not fit every phrase. Learn related words in useful sentences, not only as pairs.",example:"“Tiny” and “small” are synonyms; “tiny” sounds stronger and may not fit every formal phrase.",exam:"Define each relation and use a context-appropriate example sentence."},
      {title:"Abbreviations and audience",notes:"An abbreviation shortens a word or phrase. Standard forms can save space, but the reader must know them and the task must allow them. Academic answers and formal communication usually need complete, clear wording; informal chat shortcuts may look careless there. Use punctuation and capitalization in the conventional form expected by your course.",example:"“Dr.” is widely recognized in a name; an unfamiliar text-message shortcut may confuse an examiner.",exam:"Use abbreviations only when they are standard and appropriate to the audience."},
      {title:"A practical vocabulary-building routine",notes:"Record each new word with a simple definition, a sentence of your own and a related word or word family. Review by covering the definition and recalling it, then use the word in a new sentence. Context helps: clues around an unfamiliar word may include an example, contrast or explanation. This turns a word list into usable vocabulary.",example:"Word: “reluctant.” Meaning: not eager. Sentence: “I was reluctant to guess, so I checked the evidence.” Related: reluctance.",exam:"Show meaning, context and correct use rather than repeating a memorized list."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Phrases, clauses and sentence fragments",notes:"A phrase is a group of related words without a complete subject–verb idea. A clause contains a subject and a verb. An independent clause expresses a complete thought; a dependent clause needs another clause. A fragment may be only a phrase or dependent clause presented as a full sentence, so check who or what acts and what happens.",example:"“Because the bus was late” is dependent; “Because the bus was late, Mira walked” is complete.",exam:"Underline subject and verb, then decide whether the idea can stand alone."},
      {title:"Simple, compound and complex sentences",notes:"A simple sentence has one independent clause. A compound sentence joins two independent clauses, often with a coordinator such as and, but or so. A complex sentence pairs an independent clause with at least one dependent clause. Sentence variety can show relationships between ideas, but avoid joining unrelated thoughts into a run-on sentence.",example:"Simple: “We revised.” Compound: “We revised, and we solved a paper.” Complex: “We revised because the exam was near.”",exam:"Identify the clauses and punctuation or conjunction that joins them."},
      {title:"The shape of a clear paragraph",notes:"A paragraph usually develops one main idea. A topic sentence states the focus; supporting sentences explain it with reasons, facts or examples; a closing or linking sentence completes the point or leads forward. Keep details that support the focus and move unrelated ideas elsewhere. A paragraph's length depends on the task, but its focus should remain clear.",example:"Topic: exercise supports health. Support: it strengthens the heart and can reduce tension. Close by connecting both effects.",exam:"Mark the topic sentence, support and closing sentence in your answer."},
      {title:"Coherence and logical order",notes:"Coherence means readers can follow how one sentence leads to the next. Arrange ideas by time, importance, comparison or cause and effect. Repeat important terms when a pronoun could be unclear, and use transitions to show the relationship. Remove a sentence that does not support the paragraph's central idea.",example:"First explain a problem, next its cause, then the effect and finally a solution.",exam:"State the organizing order and use transitions that match the relationship."},
      {title:"Precise wording and punctuation",notes:"Precise verbs and nouns say more with fewer words. Remove fillers that do not add meaning, but keep enough explanation for the reader. Punctuation marks sentence boundaries and relationships: a comma cannot usually join two complete sentences by itself. Read aloud to notice where a sentence runs too long or stops before its thought is complete.",example:"Replace “made a decision to improve” with “decided to improve” when the meaning stays the same.",exam:"Choose clear words, complete sentences and punctuation that makes clause boundaries visible."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Subject–verb agreement",notes:"A verb agrees with its subject in number and person. Singular “each” takes a singular verb: “Each student has a book.” Extra words between subject and verb can distract; find the subject first. In “The box of pencils is here,” box is the subject. Collective or indefinite subjects can follow course-specific conventions, so use the rule being tested.",example:"“The list of names is ready,” because list is the subject, not names.",exam:"Locate the subject, ignore intervening phrases, then select the matching verb."},
      {title:"Pronouns and clear reference",notes:"A pronoun replaces a noun phrase, so its reader should know what it refers to. Pronouns also have grammatical roles: subject forms such as they and object forms such as them. Possessives show ownership. Singular “they” is common for an unknown person; the verb still agrees with the singular antecedent: “Each student has submitted their work.”",example:"“The teacher praised them” uses them as the object; “they praised the teacher” uses they as the subject.",exam:"Check the pronoun's reference, number and role in the sentence."},
      {title:"Articles: a, an and the",notes:"A and an introduce a nonspecific singular countable noun; choose between them by the first sound, not simply the first letter. The points to a particular item the reader can identify. Some nouns take no article in general statements. Article choice depends on meaning and whether the item is new, general or already known.",example:"“A university” starts with a /y/ sound; “an hour” starts with a vowel sound. “The book” means a specific book.",exam:"Explain whether the noun is general, first-mentioned or specific."},
      {title:"Verb tense and preposition choices",notes:"Verb tense places an action in time and should stay consistent unless the time changes. Prepositions show relationships such as place, time or direction, but many occur in fixed phrases that must be learned through examples. In proofreading, check that tense matches the intended timeline and that the chosen preposition makes the relationship clear.",example:"“She studied yesterday and is revising today” changes tense because the time changes.",exam:"Identify the time intended and check the verb/preposition pattern in context."},
      {title:"Modifiers and sentence order",notes:"A modifier describes another word. It should sit close enough that the reader can tell what it describes. A misplaced modifier can accidentally attach to the wrong subject, while a dangling modifier has no clear word to describe. Put the person or thing doing the action beside the opening phrase.",example:"Unclear: “Walking to class, the rain soaked Maya.” Clear: “Walking to class, Maya was soaked by the rain.”",exam:"Ask who performs the action in each opening phrase."},
      {title:"Redundancy, homophones and layered proofreading",notes:"Redundancy repeats meaning without adding information, as in “advance planning.” Homophones sound alike but differ in spelling and meaning, such as there, their and they're. Proofread in layers: first check the message and sentence structure, then agreement and tense, then articles, prepositions, word forms, spelling and punctuation. Spell-check alone cannot choose the correct meaning.",example:"“They left their bags there” uses their for ownership and there for a place.",exam:"Name the error, explain why it is wrong, then write the corrected sentence."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Descriptive writing",notes:"Descriptive writing helps a reader picture a person, place, object or event. Select details that matter to the purpose, then arrange them by space, sequence or importance. Specific observations are stronger than a long list of general adjectives. Use sensory detail where it helps, while keeping the description organized and relevant.",example:"Describe a classroom from the doorway inward: rows of desks, a bright board and the sound of a fan.",exam:"State the order you use and include selected details that support the subject."},
      {title:"Definitional writing",notes:"A definition identifies a term and explains the features that distinguish it from related ideas. A useful paragraph can give the category, distinguishing features, examples and significance. Broad terms such as success need a clear scope because different people may define them differently. Avoid a circular definition that merely repeats the word.",example:"Define a “reliable source” by its evidence, author expertise, transparency and relevance to the question.",exam:"Define the term, explain its boundaries and support it with an example."},
      {title:"Classification writing",notes:"Classification divides a larger group into categories using a stated principle. Begin by naming the whole group and the basis for sorting it. Explain each category with parallel wording and an example. Categories should not overlap confusingly or leave out a major group if the task expects a complete classification.",example:"Classify revision by reviewing content, practising retrieval and applying ideas to new problems.",exam:"State the sorting rule first, then describe each group consistently."},
      {title:"Introductions, body paragraphs and conclusions",notes:"An introduction presents the topic and direction, often with a central claim or definition. Body paragraphs develop parts of that direction, each with a clear role and supporting detail. A conclusion answers the prompt and closes the discussion; it can restate the main idea in fresh words and explain why it matters. Do not introduce a new major argument at the end.",example:"An essay on success might define the term, discuss two measures of it, then conclude with the definition supported by the examples.",exam:"Make sure every section helps answer the prompt from beginning to end."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Reading comprehension",notes:"Comprehension asks you to understand a passage, not just find matching words. Read once for the overall message and again for the main idea, supporting details, tone and structure. Use nearby words to infer an unfamiliar term. When answering, point to evidence in the passage and separate the author's view from your own.",example:"If a passage contrasts two plans, identify the comparison before answering which plan the author supports.",exam:"Answer the exact question and use relevant details from the passage."},
      {title:"What a precis does",notes:"A precis is a concise version of a passage that keeps its central claim and important supporting ideas. It removes illustrations, repetition and minor details, but preserves the author's meaning and logical order. It should be objective, coherent and written in your own words. Follow any word limit or ratio given in the exam.",example:"A long passage about three causes of pollution can become one concise statement preserving all three causes and the author's conclusion.",exam:"Do not add opinions or copy sentences as a substitute for summarizing."},
      {title:"Steps for writing a precis",notes:"Read for the whole message, then mark the thesis and necessary support. Cross out examples, repeated points and details that do not carry the argument. Draft a shorter version in your own words, keeping the order of ideas. Check accuracy, cohesion, objectivity and length; add a title only when requested.",example:"After drafting, compare each sentence with the source and ask whether it preserves a main point or adds something new.",exam:"Show a clear process: understand, select, condense, rewrite and check."},
      {title:"Planning a focused essay",notes:"Unpack the prompt and decide exactly what it asks: explain, compare, discuss or argue. Write a thesis that answers it. Plan an introduction, two or three body points with examples, and a conclusion that follows from those points. One main idea per paragraph helps the reader see the reasoning.",example:"For responsible technology use, plan access to learning, risks of distraction, and a conclusion about purposeful use.",exam:"Spend a few minutes outlining; keep every paragraph tied to the prompt."},
      {title:"Timed writing and revision",notes:"Under time pressure, reserve time for planning and a final check rather than writing until the last second. Draft complete paragraphs with topic sentences and relevant support. During revision, first check whether the answer addresses the prompt; then check sentence boundaries, agreement, tense, punctuation and word choice. A short, clear answer is stronger than an unfocused one.",example:"Use a final minute to verify that the conclusion follows from the body and no paragraph answers a different question.",exam:"Budget time to plan, write and review."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Pronunciation and articulation",notes:"Pronunciation concerns how speech sounds and words are produced. Clear articulation helps listeners distinguish sounds and words. Accent differences are normal; the communication goal is understandable speech suited to the listener and setting. Practising difficult words slowly, then in a sentence, helps connect sound to natural speech.",example:"Say a new technical word slowly, break it into syllables, then use it in a full sentence.",exam:"Explain pronunciation as sound production and connect it to intelligibility."},
      {title:"Word stress and sentence stress",notes:"Stress gives a syllable or word extra prominence. In a word, changing which syllable is stressed can change how it sounds and sometimes its word class. In a sentence, stress can show which information is new or contrasted. Stress is not simply shouting; it combines emphasis, duration and pitch.",example:"“I wanted the BLUE notebook” stresses blue to contrast its color with another one.",exam:"Define stress and explain how it can highlight a syllable or key word."},
      {title:"Rhythm and intonation",notes:"Rhythm is the pattern of stressed and unstressed syllables in connected speech. Intonation is the movement of pitch across a phrase or sentence. Together they help listeners hear focus, contrast, a question, a conclusion or uncertainty. Effective speech uses these features naturally and clearly rather than keeping every word equally strong.",example:"A rising pitch at the end can suggest a question; a falling pitch may mark a finished statement.",exam:"Keep rhythm (stress pattern) distinct from intonation (pitch movement)."},
      {title:"Listening and conversation skills",notes:"Good conversation includes listening fully, noticing the speaker's point and asking relevant follow-up questions. Let others finish before responding. If a message is unclear, ask for clarification rather than guessing. Respectful turn-taking helps people exchange ideas and prevents one voice from taking over.",example:"“You mentioned the survey changed the result. What changed in the questions?” is a focused follow-up.",exam:"Describe attentive listening, relevant questions and avoiding interruption."},
      {title:"Workplace communication and presentations",notes:"Workplace communication should be respectful, concise and suited to the listener. A formal presentation has a purpose and planned structure: introduce the topic, signpost sections, explain a few points with examples and finish with a summary or action. A casual conversation is more flexible, but both require clear speech and attention to the audience.",example:"A presentation can say “First, the problem; next, the evidence; finally, our recommendation” to guide listeners.",exam:"Compare planned structure and audience adaptation with informal conversation."}
    ]
  ],
  iks: [
    [
      {title:"The four Vedas",notes:"The four Vedas named in the syllabus are Rigveda, Yajurveda, Samaveda and Atharvaveda. They are foundational bodies of textual tradition and are associated with different collections and forms of material. For this introductory unit, learn the names accurately and use the explanations given in your course rather than assuming all four contain the same kind of text.",example:"Write the four names in sequence, then identify them collectively as the Vedas.",exam:"List all four without substituting a philosophical school or Vedanga."},
      {title:"The six Vedangas",notes:"Vedangas are supporting disciplines associated with studying and transmitting Vedic texts. Shiksha concerns sound and pronunciation; Kalpa ritual procedures; Vyakarana grammar; Nirukta explanation of difficult words; Chandas metre; and Jyotisha timekeeping and calendrical matters. The names are easier to remember when paired with their function.",example:"If a question asks how to understand a word, Nirukta is relevant; if it asks about metre, Chandas is relevant.",exam:"Name each of the six and pair it with its subject."},
      {title:"Philosophical traditions and schools",notes:"The guide groups Samkhya, Yoga, Nyaya, Vaisheshika, Purva-Mimamsa and Vedanta as Vedic schools, and Charvaka, Buddhist and Jain traditions as non-Vedic. Their views differ on knowledge, reality, self, causation and liberation. Treat the grouping as the syllabus presents it, then distinguish schools by their questions rather than describing one unified viewpoint.",example:"Nyaya is associated with reasoning and means of knowledge; Yoga discusses disciplined practice and liberation within its own tradition.",exam:"State the course grouping, then give one careful distinguishing concern."},
      {title:"Itihasa, Puranic and niti literature",notes:"In this course, Itihasa commonly refers to the Ramayana and Mahabharata. Puranic literature and niti literature are distinct genres with different ways of presenting stories, traditions, conduct or guidance. Their purposes should not be collapsed into one category. When you discuss a text, name its genre and use the context taught in class.",example:"An answer can distinguish an Itihasa narrative from niti material concerned with conduct or practical guidance.",exam:"Name the genre and explain its role instead of listing texts alone."},
      {title:"Caturdasa Vidyasthana and 64 Kalas",notes:"Caturdasa Vidyasthana and the 64 Kalas are syllabus concepts for classifying bodies of learning and arts. The precise contents and arrangement should follow the course's list, since traditions and sources can organize these categories differently. Use them to show that knowledge was discussed through named disciplines and arts, not as one undivided subject.",example:"Organize your class list into its named learning areas or arts before memorizing individual examples.",exam:"Use the exact classifications and examples included in your course notes."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Panini's Ashtadhyayi and formal rules",notes:"Panini's Ashtadhyayi is a highly organized grammatical work. It presents linguistic rules in a concise, structured way, which makes it useful to discuss how formal rules can describe complex patterns. A comparison with computation can help illustrate rule-based organization, but it is an analogy; the text should not be described as a modern programming language.",example:"A grammar rule can describe how a word changes in a context, just as a formal rule system transforms symbols.",exam:"Name the work, describe its structured rules and state the limits of a modern analogy."},
      {title:"Place value and zero",notes:"In a positional system, a digit's value depends on both its symbol and its place. In 352, 3 means hundreds, 5 tens and 2 ones. Zero can be a number and a placeholder: it shows an empty place in 205, separating it from 25. Positional notation makes large numbers compact and arithmetic systematic.",example:"205 = 2×100 + 0×10 + 5×1; the zero keeps the tens place visible.",exam:"Show a place-value expansion and explain zero's placeholder role."},
      {title:"Bhuta-Sankhya and Katapayadi",notes:"Bhuta-Sankhya and Katapayadi are number-encoding conventions that represent values through words, sounds or symbols according to mappings. They can make numbers memorable or embed them in verses, but the decoding depends on the convention being used. Learn the mapping and direction from the class example; do not assume every word has one universal numeric value.",example:"To decode a course example, first identify its stated word-to-number mapping, then apply it in the required order.",exam:"State the mapping rule before decoding or encoding an example."},
      {title:"Pingala, metre and patterns",notes:"Pingala's Chandah Sastra discusses patterns of short and long syllables in metre. Such patterns can be represented combinatorially: each position can have one of two syllable lengths, giving a structured set of combinations. This creates a useful comparison with binary patterns, but the historical topic is metre and prosody, so keep that setting explicit.",example:"For two syllable positions, the patterns are short-short, short-long, long-short and long-long.",exam:"Connect the two choices per position to patterns while naming the metrical context."},
      {title:"Pramana, prameya and samshaya",notes:"In the Vaisheshika terminology used in the guide, pramana is a means or source of valid knowledge, prameya is the object to be known, and samshaya is doubt about what is the case. These terms describe parts of a knowledge process: something is to be known, a means is used to know it, and uncertainty may need resolution.",example:"Seeing a distant shape creates samshaya; careful observation is a pramana used to decide what the prameya is.",exam:"Define each term and show how they relate in one example."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Sulba Sutras and practical geometry",notes:"The Sulba Sutras preserve geometric procedures associated with constructing ritual spaces and altars. The material connects measurement, construction and transformation of shapes to a specific historical and ritual setting. Explain the procedure and its purpose as presented in the course. Do not equate a short traditional rule with a modern proof unless the source supports that comparison.",example:"A construction problem may require transferring a measured length or making a new altar shape with a prescribed area.",exam:"Name the construction context, describe a geometric method and keep claims evidence-based."},
      {title:"Mathematicians and mathematical subjects",notes:"The guide names Aryabhata and the Aryabhatiya, Brahmagupta and Bhaskara, alongside arithmetic, geometry, algebra, trigonometry and pi. Learn each scholar with the work or topic your class associates with them. A strong historical answer explains the mathematical problem or method, not only a list of famous names.",example:"Pair Aryabhata with the Aryabhatiya, then describe the mathematical or astronomical context taught in class.",exam:"Use the course's names, works and examples precisely."},
      {title:"Pingala and combinatorial patterns",notes:"Pingala's Chandah Sastra connects this unit's mathematical ideas with patterns of syllable lengths in poetic metre. Short and long syllables create combinations that can be counted or ordered. This is a way to study structured patterns in a historical text; explain the connection carefully without claiming it is identical to modern binary computing.",example:"With three syllable positions and two choices at each, there are 2×2×2 possible patterns.",exam:"Show the pattern or count and name the prosodic setting."},
      {title:"The five parts of a Panchanga",notes:"A Panchanga organizes calendrical information through five limbs: tithi (lunar day), vara (weekday), nakshatra (lunar mansion), yoga (a calculated angular relationship) and karana (half a tithi). Each contributes a different time or sky measure. Together they form a traditional calendar framework, rather than simply a list of dates.",example:"For a complete answer, write each term followed by its meaning in the same order.",exam:"Name all five and define each briefly."},
      {title:"Jantar Mantar and astronomical instruments",notes:"Jantar Mantar observatories used large instruments for observing and calculating astronomical positions. The instruments linked physical design with observation of the sky and calendrical calculation. Discuss the observatory in its historical setting, name the kind of observation or calculation, and avoid suggesting that a historical instrument answers every question addressed by modern astronomy.",example:"An instrument designed around a fixed geometric alignment can help observe a celestial position at a given time.",exam:"Connect the instrument, observation and historical purpose."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Metals and material knowledge",notes:"The guide names copper, gold, zinc, mercury, lead and silver, and asks students to discuss historical uses and production methods. A useful account connects a material to its properties, practical use and craft process. The exact date, region or technique should come from the class source; avoid making sweeping claims about every period or community.",example:"Describe one metal by naming a documented use, how it was worked and who used or traded the object.",exam:"Pair the material and use with a supported historical context."},
      {title:"Iron, steel and process steps",notes:"Iron and steel history can involve ore selection, furnaces, heating, smelting, alloying, shaping, tools and trade. Each step requires knowledge of materials and heat. A careful answer uses the specific process documented for the region or example being studied instead of suggesting one universal technique or unsupported superiority.",example:"An answer can trace ore preparation → heating/smelting → shaping → finishing, then connect the product to tools or trade.",exam:"Explain the sequence and identify the example's time or place when known."},
      {title:"Dyes, pigments and painting",notes:"Dye and painting technologies combine material choices with preparation and application. A colorant must be prepared, applied to a suitable surface and, depending on the material, fixed or protected. Craftspeople's techniques vary with cloth, wall, pigment and region. Use the examples in class and describe how material knowledge supports the artistic result.",example:"A painting answer can identify the surface, color material and application technique shown in the course source.",exam:"Connect material, process and craft purpose."},
      {title:"Town planning and temple architecture",notes:"Town planning and architecture involve site, layout, materials, structures and movement through space. Temple forms can include a sanctum, tower or superstructure, halls, gateways and sculpture, but regional traditions differ. Discuss how form and layout relate to function and symbolism, and name a style or region when the source provides it.",example:"A plan may guide movement from an outer entrance toward a more enclosed sanctum.",exam:"Describe major elements and acknowledge variation across regions and traditions."},
      {title:"Vastu Shastra in historical context",notes:"Vastu Shastra is a body of architectural principles and texts that discuss built space and design. Explain the concepts and terminology as historical knowledge from the course. Claims about a particular design should be tied to evidence; a traditional principle should not automatically be presented as proof of modern engineering performance.",example:"Discuss a layout principle from the course as a historical design idea, then distinguish it from a modern structural calculation.",exam:"Describe the tradition accurately and separate historical concepts from engineering evidence."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Food, water, sleep and wellbeing",notes:"The unit considers how daily habits such as food, water, rest and routine relate to health in Ayurveda and yoga traditions. Explain the framework or practice as the course presents it and give the historical or conceptual context. Do not turn a short syllabus idea into individualized medical advice or treat all traditions as identical.",example:"A course answer can describe why routine and balanced habits are emphasized, then name the tradition being discussed.",exam:"Identify the framework and explain the idea without making unsupported medical claims."},
      {title:"Yoga includes practices and philosophies",notes:"Yoga is not only posture. The syllabus presents it as a set of practices and philosophies that can include discipline, attention, ethical conduct and paths toward liberation. A good answer names the specific path or practice being discussed and explains its purpose in that tradition, rather than using the word yoga as if it referred to only one exercise.",example:"If discussing meditation, state the practice and its role rather than describing it as a posture.",exam:"Name the path or practice and use the course's context."},
      {title:"Triguna: sattva, rajas and tamas",notes:"The Triguna framework describes qualities: sattva is associated with clarity and balance, rajas with activity and desire, and tamas with inertia or obscurity. These ideas are used within particular philosophical traditions to discuss mind and nature. They are a conceptual framework, not measured modern personality categories or clinical diagnoses.",example:"A focused, calm state can illustrate sattva; restless activity can illustrate rajas; heaviness or inertia can illustrate tamas.",exam:"Define all three and identify the framework as a traditional concept."},
      {title:"Ethics, duty and the Ramayana",notes:"The Ramayana is discussed in the course as a text that can offer perspectives on duty, conduct and social roles. Interpret examples in the story's literary and historical setting. A character's action can prompt ethical discussion, but a single episode should not be treated as a universal rule without explaining context and the interpretive lens used.",example:"An answer can describe a duty conflict in a narrative, then explain the values it raises.",exam:"Use a named episode or idea and explain its context before interpreting it."},
      {title:"Arthashastra and statecraft",notes:"The Arthashastra is a classical text associated with administration and statecraft. The course points to topics such as rulers, officials, revenue, law, diplomacy and security. A careful answer explains the text's practical orientation and historical setting. It should not equate its prescriptions directly with present-day constitutional or democratic institutions.",example:"Discuss revenue administration as one topic, then distinguish the text's setting from a current public finance system.",exam:"Name a governance topic and keep historical prescription separate from current law."},
      {title:"Historical knowledge and present-day institutions",notes:"When comparing an older text or framework with modern society, first describe the historical idea accurately. Then state the point of comparison and the important difference in context, values or institutions. This avoids treating past and present as interchangeable and helps an answer show both understanding and judgment.",example:"Compare a classical account of administration with a modern institution by naming one shared question and one structural difference.",exam:"Use context, comparison and limitation in that order."}
    ]
  ],
  math: [
    [
      {title:"Sets and set operations",notes:"A set is a well-defined collection of distinct objects called elements. The union A∪B contains elements in A or B; the intersection A∩B contains elements in both; and A−B contains elements in A but not B. The complement depends on a stated universal set. Venn diagrams can show these relationships, but define the sets and universe before interpreting a shaded region.",example:"If A={1,2,3} and B={3,4}, then A∩B={3} and A∪B={1,2,3,4}.",exam:"State membership carefully and use braces for set elements."},
      {title:"Cartesian products and ordered pairs",notes:"The Cartesian product A×B contains every ordered pair (a,b) with a in A and b in B. Order matters: (a,b) is generally different from (b,a). If A has m elements and B has n, then A×B has mn ordered pairs. A relation from A to B is any subset of this product.",example:"For A={1,2} and B={x,y}, A×B={(1,x),(1,y),(2,x),(2,y)}.",exam:"List pairs in the correct order and count them with |A×B|=|A||B|."},
      {title:"Relations and their properties",notes:"A relation R on A is a set of ordered pairs from A×A. Reflexive means every element is related to itself. Symmetric means aRb implies bRa. Transitive means aRb and bRc together imply aRc. Test each property using its definition; a counterexample is enough to show that a property fails.",example:"“Is a sibling of” is usually symmetric, but it is not reflexive because a person is not their own sibling.",exam:"Check reflexive, symmetric and transitive one by one."},
      {title:"Equivalence relations and classes",notes:"An equivalence relation is reflexive, symmetric and transitive. It groups elements that count as equivalent under a chosen rule. The equivalence class of a contains every element related to a; classes partition the set, so each element belongs to one class and distinct classes do not overlap. Congruence modulo n groups integers by their remainder after division by n.",example:"For aRb when 3 divides a−b, integers are grouped into remainder classes 0, 1 and 2 modulo 3.",exam:"Prove the three properties, then identify the resulting classes."},
      {title:"Functions: injective, surjective and bijective",notes:"A function assigns each domain input exactly one output in the codomain. It may send different inputs to the same output. It is injective (one-to-one) if different inputs always have different outputs; surjective (onto) if every codomain value is reached; bijective if both. Check the stated domain and codomain before classifying a function.",example:"f(x)=x² from real numbers to real numbers is not injective because f(2)=f(−2), and it is not onto because negative outputs are missing.",exam:"Use the definitions and state the domain/codomain."},
      {title:"Warshall's transitive-closure algorithm",notes:"Warshall's algorithm finds reachability in a directed graph. Start with an adjacency matrix M. For each possible intermediate vertex k, update each entry: M[i,j] = M[i,j] OR (M[i,k] AND M[k,j]). A 1 then means i can reach j using the allowed intermediate vertices. Decide whether the diagonal should include zero-length self-reachability, as the question specifies.",example:"If edge 1→2 and edge 2→3 exist, considering k=2 adds reachability 1→3.",exam:"Show the matrix after each k and state how self-reachability is treated."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Addition and multiplication principles",notes:"The addition principle counts mutually exclusive alternatives by adding their possibilities. The multiplication principle counts a sequence of choices by multiplying the number of options at each stage. First decide whether a task is “this choice or that one” or “this choice and then another.” Avoid multiplying overlapping alternatives or adding stages that happen together.",example:"Choose one of 3 shirts and one of 2 hats: 3×2=6 outfits. Choose a shirt or a hat as one gift: 3+2=5 options.",exam:"Describe the choice structure before writing the calculation."},
      {title:"Permutations: order matters",notes:"A permutation counts ordered arrangements. For n distinct items selected r at a time without repetition, nPr=n!/(n−r)!. Each position has fewer options after an item is used. Use permutations for roles, rankings or codes where changing order creates a different outcome. Adjust the method if repetition is allowed or some items are identical.",example:"Four ordered letters from eight distinct letters: 8P4=8×7×6×5=1,680.",exam:"Explain why different orders count separately, then show the formula and substitution."},
      {title:"Combinations: order does not matter",notes:"A combination counts selections where only the chosen group matters. For n distinct items chosen r at a time, nCr=n!/[r!(n−r)!]. The r! division removes the repeated orderings of the same group. Combinations are useful for selecting committees, teams or subsets without assigning positions.",example:"Choosing 2 students from 4 gives 4C2=6 groups; swapping the same two students does not create another group.",exam:"State that order is irrelevant and show how repeated arrangements are removed."},
      {title:"Pigeonhole principle",notes:"If more objects are placed into fewer boxes, at least one box must contain two or more objects. The basic form needs no probability: it is guaranteed by counting. A stronger form says that placing N objects into k boxes makes some box hold at least ceiling(N/k) objects. Identify the objects and boxes in an application.",example:"Among 13 people and 12 birth months, at least two people share a birth month.",exam:"Name the objects and categories, then compare their counts."},
      {title:"Binomial theorem",notes:"The binomial theorem expands (x+y)^n as the sum of C(n,r)x^(n−r)y^r from r=0 to n. The coefficients are combinations because each term counts the ways to choose which r of the n factors contribute y. Powers of x decrease as powers of y increase, and each term's exponents add to n.",example:"(x+y)^3 = x³ + 3x²y + 3xy² + y³.",exam:"Write terms in descending x power and check every term has total degree n."},
      {title:"Recurrence relations and characteristic roots",notes:"A recurrence defines each sequence term using earlier terms; initial values identify the particular sequence. For a linear recurrence with constant coefficients, try a solution aₙ=rⁿ and substitute it to form a characteristic equation. Solve for roots, write the general solution, then use the initial values to solve for constants. Repeated roots require a modified form.",example:"aₙ=3aₙ₋₁−2aₙ₋₂ gives r²−3r+2=0, with roots 1 and 2.",exam:"Show the characteristic equation, roots and use of every initial condition."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Vertices, edges, degree and graph types",notes:"A graph consists of vertices (nodes) and edges (connections). The degree of a vertex is the number of incident edges; a loop contributes twice in common undirected-graph conventions. Graphs may be directed or undirected, simple or allow multiple edges, connected or disconnected. A diagram makes these properties visible, but use the definitions when giving an answer.",example:"In a road network, intersections are vertices and road segments are edges.",exam:"Define the graph's objects and state whether edges have direction."},
      {title:"Paths, trails and cycles",notes:"A walk is a sequence of adjacent vertices and edges. A trail does not repeat edges; a path usually does not repeat vertices. A cycle returns to its starting vertex without repeating intermediate vertices. Terminology can vary slightly by text, so use the course's definitions and show the route on a diagram.",example:"A-B-C is a path if edges AB and BC exist; A-B-C-A is a cycle if CA also exists.",exam:"Mark the route and say what may or may not repeat."},
      {title:"Trees and the n−1 edge rule",notes:"A tree is a connected, acyclic graph. Every pair of vertices has exactly one path between them. A tree with n vertices has n−1 edges: adding a new vertex while preserving connectivity and avoiding cycles requires one new edge. Conversely, a connected graph with n−1 edges is a tree. The rule is a quick check, not a substitute for verifying connectedness when needed.",example:"A tree with 6 vertices has 5 edges.",exam:"State both properties—connected and acyclic—before using the edge count."},
      {title:"Spanning trees",notes:"A spanning tree is a subgraph that includes every vertex of a connected graph but has no cycles. It keeps just enough edges to maintain connectivity, so with n vertices it has n−1 edges. Spanning trees are useful for connecting network locations without redundant links. A graph can have several different spanning trees.",example:"A network with roads forming loops can remove some roads and still connect every town as a spanning tree.",exam:"Show that every vertex is included, the subgraph is connected and there are no cycles."},
      {title:"Euler and Hamiltonian circuits",notes:"An Euler circuit uses every edge exactly once and returns to its starting vertex; vertices may be revisited. For a connected undirected graph, it exists exactly when every vertex has even degree. A Hamiltonian cycle visits every vertex exactly once before returning to its start; it does not have to use every edge. Euler is about edges; Hamiltonian is about vertices.",example:"A route that checks every road once is Euler-like; a tour that visits every city once is Hamiltonian-like.",exam:"Underline whether the condition refers to edges or vertices."},
      {title:"Planar graphs and Euler's formula",notes:"A planar graph can be drawn on a plane without edges crossing except at shared vertices. For a connected planar graph, V−E+F=2, where V is vertices, E edges and F faces, including the unbounded outside region. Different drawings of the same planar graph can have different-looking regions, but the formula still holds.",example:"A triangle has V=3, E=3 and F=2 (inside and outside), so 3−3+2=2.",exam:"Count the outside face and substitute into the formula."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Determinants and invertibility",notes:"For a 2×2 matrix [[a,b],[c,d]], det(A)=ad−bc. A nonzero determinant means the square matrix is invertible; zero means its rows or columns are dependent and the matrix is singular. Determinants also appear in solving systems and finding eigenvalues. Keep the sign and order of entries consistent during calculation.",example:"For [[2,1],[1,2]], determinant is 2×2−1×1=3, so an inverse exists.",exam:"Calculate the determinant first and use it to justify whether an inverse exists."},
      {title:"Inverse of a 2×2 matrix",notes:"For A=[[a,b],[c,d]] with nonzero determinant, A⁻¹=(1/(ad−bc))[[d,−b],[−c,a]]. The diagonal entries swap and the off-diagonal entries change signs, then the matrix is divided by the determinant. The inverse reverses the transformation because AA⁻¹=I. Always verify by multiplication when time permits.",example:"For [[2,1],[1,2]], det=3 and inverse is (1/3)[[2,−1],[−1,2]].",exam:"Show determinant, adjugate pattern and scalar division."},
      {title:"Rank and row reduction",notes:"Rank is the number of linearly independent rows or columns; row rank and column rank are equal. To find it, use elementary row operations to reduce the matrix to echelon form and count nonzero rows. Rank describes how much independent information a matrix contains and helps analyze whether a system has enough independent equations.",example:"If reduction leaves two nonzero rows, the rank is 2.",exam:"Show row operations clearly and count nonzero rows in the reduced form."},
      {title:"Eigenvalues and eigenvectors",notes:"An eigenvector is a nonzero vector whose direction is unchanged by a square matrix; the matrix scales it by its eigenvalue λ: Av=λv. Rearranging gives (A−λI)v=0. A nonzero solution exists when det(A−λI)=0; solve this characteristic equation for eigenvalues, then solve for each corresponding eigenvector.",example:"For [[2,1],[1,2]], eigenvalues are 3 and 1; directions (1,1) and (1,−1) are corresponding eigenvectors.",exam:"Find roots of det(A−λI), then solve the homogeneous system for each λ."},
      {title:"Cramer's rule",notes:"Cramer's rule solves a square linear system when the determinant D of the coefficient matrix is nonzero. To find xᵢ, replace the i-th coefficient column with the constants column to get Dᵢ, then xᵢ=Dᵢ/D. It is convenient for small systems but requires a nonzero determinant and becomes inefficient for large systems.",example:"For x+y=5 and 2x−y=1, D=−3, Dₓ=−6, Dᵧ=−9, so x=2 and y=3.",exam:"Show D, each replaced-column determinant and the final check."},
      {title:"Cayley-Hamilton theorem",notes:"The Cayley-Hamilton theorem states that every square matrix satisfies its own characteristic polynomial. If the characteristic polynomial is p(λ), then substituting A gives p(A)=0. This can reduce high powers of a matrix to lower powers. If A is invertible, the relation can also be rearranged to express A⁻¹ in terms of lower powers, following the exact polynomial.",example:"If p(λ)=λ²−3λ+2, the theorem gives A²−3A+2I=0 for that matrix.",exam:"Find the characteristic polynomial first, then replace λ with A and simplify."}
    ]
  ],
  'problem-solving': [
    [
      {title:"Problem, instance and specification",notes:"A problem is the general task; an instance supplies particular input values. A specification states allowed inputs, required outputs, constraints and special cases. Before coding, restate the task in plain language and identify what counts as a correct result. A clear specification prevents the algorithm from solving a nearby but different problem.",example:"Problem: find the largest value. Instance: values 7, 3 and 9. Output: 9.",exam:"List input, output, constraints and edge cases before the algorithm."},
      {title:"Preconditions and postconditions",notes:"A precondition describes what must be true before an algorithm begins, such as “the array is sorted.” A postcondition describes what must be true after it finishes, such as “the target index is returned if found.” These statements make assumptions visible and give a way to reason about correctness.",example:"Binary search precondition: the array is sorted. Postcondition: return a matching index or report not found.",exam:"State assumptions up front and describe what is guaranteed at the end."},
      {title:"Algorithm characteristics",notes:"An algorithm is a finite, ordered, unambiguous set of steps that transforms specified input into required output. It should terminate, be precise enough to execute, produce correct results for valid input and use feasible time and memory. Correctness comes first; then improve efficiency without making the steps unclear.",example:"To find the larger of a and b: read both, compare them, and output the greater value; define what happens on a tie.",exam:"Explain finiteness, definiteness, input/output and correctness."},
      {title:"Decomposition and stepwise refinement",notes:"A large task becomes easier when split into smaller subproblems with clear inputs and outputs. Solve a main task by combining those pieces, then refine vague steps until each is precise enough to execute. This also makes code easier to build and debug because each function or section has a focused job.",example:"A student-score report can be split into reading scores, validating them, calculating totals and printing results.",exam:"Name the subproblems and show how their results combine."},
      {title:"Pseudocode and flowcharts",notes:"Pseudocode describes an algorithm in structured, language-neutral steps. A flowchart uses standard shapes and arrows to show sequence, decisions and loops. Both help plan before writing C. They should make branches and repetition explicit, including what happens when a condition is false and when the algorithm stops.",example:"A decision diamond “a>b?” leads to output a on yes and output b on no.",exam:"Use consistent symbols or indentation and label both decision branches."},
      {title:"Tracing, testing and efficiency",notes:"A trace follows an algorithm's variables step by step for a chosen input. Testing checks normal cases, boundaries and unusual inputs against expected results. Efficiency considers resources such as time and memory; it matters after correctness is established. Small examples can expose a flawed condition, but a single successful case does not prove correctness for every valid input.",example:"For a maximum-finder, test one value, equal values, a maximum at the start and a maximum at the end.",exam:"Show test inputs, expected outputs and why they cover different cases."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"The three control structures",notes:"Sequence executes statements in order. Selection chooses between paths based on a condition. Repetition runs a block multiple times while a rule holds. Most structured programs are built by combining these three patterns, which makes the logic easier to read and trace than arbitrary jumps.",example:"Read a mark (sequence), choose pass/fail (selection), repeat for each student (repetition).",exam:"Identify the control structure and explain its role in the algorithm."},
      {title:"if/else and switch",notes:"Use if/else when a condition such as mark>=40 selects between outcomes; conditions can be combined or nested. Use switch for a discrete choice among fixed values such as menu options. In C, each switch case generally needs a break to avoid falling through to the next case; include a default branch for unlisted values.",example:"A menu can switch on choice 1, 2 or 3 and display a message for default.",exam:"Show the condition or cases, all outcomes and the fallback behavior."},
      {title:"while and do-while loops",notes:"A while loop is entry-controlled: its condition is checked before the body, so it may run zero times. A do-while loop is exit-controlled: its body runs once before the condition is checked, so it runs at least once. Choose while when work may not be needed; choose do-while for a menu that must appear once.",example:"Read values while input is available with while; show a menu at least once with do-while.",exam:"State when each condition is checked and the minimum body count."},
      {title:"Loop initialization, condition and update",notes:"A safe loop has an initial state, a condition that controls repetition, a body and an update that moves toward termination. Trace the condition before and after each pass. A wrong boundary can skip an item or run one extra time; a missing update can create an infinite loop.",example:"For i=1; i<=n; i++, values 1 through n are included exactly once.",exam:"Show the initial value, loop condition, update and stopping value."},
      {title:"Sentinel-controlled input and C translation",notes:"A sentinel is a special value that ends data entry and is not part of the data. The program must read a value before testing it and avoid adding the sentinel to totals. Translate pseudocode into C in small pieces, then trace zero, one and boundary inputs. Choose a sentinel that cannot be confused with valid data, or use a separate end marker.",example:"Read x; while x!=-1, add x to sum, read the next x; stop when -1 arrives.",exam:"Explain sentinel handling, initialization, update and why it is excluded."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Extract and reverse decimal digits",notes:"For a positive integer n, n%10 gives the last decimal digit and integer n/10 removes it. Repeat until n becomes zero to process digits from right to left. To construct the reverse, update reverse=reverse×10+digit. Save the original value before changing n if it will be needed later.",example:"582 yields digits 2, 8, 5; reverse builds 0→2→28→285.",exam:"Show initialization and both remainder/division operations."},
      {title:"Palindrome and Armstrong checks",notes:"A number is a palindrome if it reads the same forward and backward; compare the reversed value with the saved original. A three-digit Armstrong number equals the sum of the cubes of its digits. The broader n-digit definition raises each digit to the number-of-digits power, so follow the version specified by the question. Be explicit about valid input and zero handling.",example:"153 = 1³+5³+3³=153, so it is a three-digit Armstrong number.",exam:"Save the original and show each digit's contribution."},
      {title:"Prime numbers and efficient divisor checks",notes:"A prime is an integer greater than 1 with exactly two positive divisors: 1 and itself. Zero, one and negative values are not prime. To test n, check possible divisors from 2 through the square root; if a larger factor exists, it must pair with a smaller one. Stop as soon as a divisor is found.",example:"For 29, test divisors through √29 (about 5.4): 2, 3, 4 and 5 do not divide it, so 29 is prime.",exam:"Handle n<=1 first, then show the divisor range and result."},
      {title:"Factorial and boundary cases",notes:"For a non-negative integer n, n!=n×(n−1)×…×1, with 0!=1. An iterative solution initializes product=1 and multiplies from 1 through n. A recursive solution uses 0 as the base case. Factorials grow quickly, so a chosen integer type may overflow for modest n; state the input range if relevant.",example:"5!=1×2×3×4×5=120; 0! is 1 by definition.",exam:"Include the 0! base case and explain the loop or recurrence."},
      {title:"Streaming statistics and safe averages",notes:"For a stream of values, update count and sum for each valid item; maintain minimum and maximum as values arrive. A sentinel ends the stream and must not be counted. Calculate average only if count>0, otherwise division by zero occurs. Initialize minimum and maximum from the first real value rather than an arbitrary constant when the value range is unknown.",example:"For 4, 9, 2: count=3, sum=15, minimum=2, maximum=9 and average=5.",exam:"Show variable initialization, sentinel exclusion and the count check."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Function prototypes, definitions and calls",notes:"A function prototype declares a function's name, return type and parameter types before it is used. A definition provides the body. A call passes arguments and may receive a return value. Functions divide a solution into focused tasks and allow reuse. Parameters and return types should match between declaration, definition and call.",example:"int square(int n); declares the function; its definition returns n*n; square(4) returns 16.",exam:"Identify prototype, definition, arguments, parameters and returned value."},
      {title:"Recursion and base cases",notes:"A recursive function calls itself with a smaller or simpler input. It needs a base case that returns without another recursive call and a recursive step that moves toward that base. Every call uses stack space, so failure to progress can cause unbounded calls and stack exhaustion. Trace calls down, then values back up.",example:"factorial(n)=1 for n=0; otherwise n×factorial(n−1).",exam:"Name the base case and prove each call approaches it."},
      {title:"Arrays and valid indexes",notes:"An array stores several values of one type in contiguous indexed positions. In C, indexing starts at 0, so an array of n items has valid indexes 0 through n−1. A loop should stop before n. Reading beyond the bounds is undefined behavior and can corrupt results; keep the length alongside the array.",example:"For int a[5], valid elements are a[0] through a[4].",exam:"State the index range and show a loop condition such as i<n."},
      {title:"Searching and sorting",notes:"Binary search compares a target with the middle of a sorted array and discards the half that cannot contain it. Each step roughly halves the search range, giving logarithmic growth in comparisons. It is not valid on unsorted data. Selection sort repeatedly finds the smallest remaining item and places it next in the sorted prefix; it is simple but uses many comparisons.",example:"Search [2,5,8,12,16] for 12: compare 8, keep the right half, then find 12.",exam:"State binary search's sorted-input requirement and show interval updates."},
      {title:"Two-dimensional arrays and matrix addition",notes:"A two-dimensional array uses a row and column index to represent a table or matrix. Nested loops visit each position. Matrix addition is defined for matrices with matching dimensions; add entries at the same row and column. Keep loop limits aligned with row and column counts and print a line break after each output row.",example:"For A=[[1,2],[3,4]] and B=[[5,6],[7,8]], A+B=[[6,8],[10,12]].",exam:"Check dimensions, use nested loops and show corresponding-entry addition."}
    ]
  ]
};
